          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"translation_en\":\"CYRANO:\\n  All those, all those, all those\\n  That come to me, I shall toss them to you in a heap,\\n  Without arranging them into a bouquet: I love you, I am suffocating,\\n  **I love you**, I am mad, I can bear no more, it is too much;\\n  Your name is in my heart as though inside a little bell,\\n  And since I am always trembling, Roxane,\\n  The bell is always stirring, and the name rings out!\\n  I remember everything about you; I have loved it all:\\n  I know that last year, one day, on the twelfth of May,\\n  You changed your hairstyle before going out that morning!\\n  I have so completely taken your hair for radiance\\n  That, as when one has stared too long at the sun\\n  And afterward sees a crimson circle upon everything,\\n  So, once I have left the blaze with which you flood me,\\n  My dazzled gaze casts golden patches over everything!\",\"larger_context_summary\":\"In the balcony scene, Cyrano speaks from the darkness to his cousin Roxane, whom he secretly loves, after the handsome but inarticulate Christian has struggled to court her successfully. Roxane believes that the eloquent declarations come from Christian, while Cyrano uses the assumed role to voice feelings and intimate observations that are genuinely his own. Christian therefore receives the immediate romantic benefit, including Roxane’s increased willingness to accept him, while Cyrano must remain concealed because he believes his appearance makes him unlovable. The scene’s stakes combine courtship, mistaken attribution, and Cyrano’s painful opportunity to speak sincerely only through another man’s identity.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The declaration primarily avows an enduring inner state, and its credibility is strengthened by Cyrano’s precise memory of Roxane’s hairstyle and by the sustained imagery of her name ringing involuntarily within him. Its expressive dimension is not merely a matter of heightened language: he explicitly presents the words as an unarranged rush, driven out by suffocation, madness, and emotional excess. That makes the speech an affective release as well as a claim about love. It has some practical effect on the courtship, but it does not itself promise marriage, impose an obligation, or clearly alter the relationship by conventional verbal force, so its performative role remains limited. The impersonation complicates attribution rather than sincerity: Roxane takes Christian to be the lover speaking, yet Cyrano’s words truthfully disclose Cyrano’s own feelings. The shift from formal “vous” to intimate “tu” further sharpens the sudden personal intensity.\"}"
        }
